In a society built on equal standing, no one's judgment—elder or otherwise—can claim automatic authority simply by virtue of role or tradition. What matters is whether the decision itself can be justified through reasons that all parties, as equals, could recognize as fair. That said, "equal voice" doesn't mean dismissing accumulated experience; the elder's judgment may still carry genuine insight worth hearing. The key equality-based principle is that authority must remain answerable to those it affects—so the younger members raising concerns is not just permissible but essential, since equal standing requires that everyone's perspective be heard and taken seriously rather than pre-empted by status. The elder's wisdom can be one input among several, but it cannot silence scrutiny.

For the reasoning question, the core equality commitment is that no authority is exempt from challenge—this follows necessarily from the idea that all persons have equal standing to question decisions that affect them. Fairness is the substantive value this protects, so it's tightly linked to that principle rather than separate from it. Traditional authority may hold real, earned insight, but this insight has to prove itself through open reasoning, not simply be presumed because of position.

