This scenario tests how a society built on equal standing handles a conflict between expertise and majority opinion. Equal standing doesn't mean expertise is irrelevant—citizens have reason to defer to specialized knowledge on technical matters. But it also doesn't mean experts get to simply override the public's judgment, since that would treat residents as subjects to be managed rather than equals capable of reasoning for themselves. The response that best honors equal standing is one that treats residents as full participants who deserve genuine engagement with the evidence, not passive recipients of expert dictates nor a majority whose views are treated as beyond scrutiny. This means the town should prioritize dialogue, transparency, and shared understanding—giving weight to expertise while respecting residents' capacity to be persuaded by good reasons, and their right not to have decisions imposed without genuine deliberation.

Between deferring wholly to experts (which risks treating citizens as incompetent to govern their own lives) and simply following the majority (which risks enshrining misinformation without addressing it), the equality-respecting path is to invest in making the reasoning behind the recommendation accessible and open to scrutiny, so that the eventual decision—whichever way it goes—reflects an informed collective judgment rather than either technocratic imposition or unexamined public sentiment.

